-- title:
--   Dual of a ψ-Whittaker function on GL₃
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ and $R$ be commutative rings and let $\psi$ be an additive character of $A$ with values in $R$. For $x,y,z \in A$ let $n(x,y,z) \in \mathrm{GL}_3(A)$ be the invertible matrix $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & x & z \\ 0 & 1 & y \\ 0 & 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$ (with explicit inverse $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & -x & xy-z \\ 0 & 1 & -y \\ 0 & 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$), as given by `upperUnipotent3`. Call a function $V : \mathrm{GL}_3(A) \to R$ a $\chi$-Whittaker function, for an additive character $\chi$ of $A$, when $V(n(x,y,z)\,g) = \chi(x+y)\,V(g)$ for all $x,y,z \in A$ and all $g \in \mathrm{GL}_3(A)$; this is the predicate `IsGL3PsiWhittakerFn`. Let $W : \mathrm{GL}_3(A) \to R$ be a function satisfying this law for $\psi$. Write $w_3 \in \mathrm{GL}_3(A)$ for the unsigned antidiagonal permutation matrix $\begin{pmatrix} 0&0&1\\0&1&0\\1&0&0\end{pmatrix}$, which is its own inverse, and ${}^{t}g^{-1}$ for the invertible matrix whose underlying matrix is the transpose of that of $g^{-1}$. The assertion is that the dual function $\widetilde W(g) = W(w_3 \, {}^{t}g^{-1})$ is a $\psi^{-1}$-Whittaker function, i.e. $\widetilde W(n(x,y,z)\,g) = \psi(-(x+y))\,\widetilde W(g)$ for all $x,y,z$ and $g$. No hypothesis is placed on $\psi$, and none on $W$ beyond the transformation law; no topology or integrability enters.
--
--   This is the purely algebraic form of the classical statement that the Whittaker space of $\mathrm{GL}_3$ is carried to that of the contragredient representation by $W \mapsto \widetilde W$, $\widetilde W(g) = W(w_3\,{}^{t}g^{-1})$, with the character replaced by its inverse. It is used in the cubic-induction development as the input that makes the dual Whittaker function an admissible vector and makes the functional equation of the local and global $\mathrm{GL}_3$ zeta integrals available, being cited for instance by the admissibility of the cyclic subspace generated by right translates of $\widetilde W$, by the simultaneous vanishing of torus-shell averages of $W$ and of its dual below a bound, and by the Laurent-expansion comparison of the local zeta integral with the $\mathrm{GL}_3$ $L$-factor polynomial.
